WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
Question 0013: sort the digits and subtract,
this time with four digits. Junior level.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.300
Three parts: check one number,
check every number, and base B.

3
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.300
In the video of question 0012, with three digits,
we got 495. Now four digits.

4
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:20.133
Take a number from 0 to 9999, say 3524,
and write it with four digits.

5
00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:24.733
Sort the digits from largest to smallest, 5432,
and from smallest to largest, 2345.

6
00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:28.233
Subtract the smaller from the larger,
and get 3087.

7
00:00:28.233 --> 00:00:31.900
If the difference equals the number itself,
the number is called special.

8
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.433
Here 3087 differs from 3524,
so 3524 is not special.

9
00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:41.433
Part A: a function that takes a number x,
and returns 1 if it is special, 0 otherwise.

10
00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:46.133
The idea: both numbers are built
from the same four digits.

11
00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:50.167
Sort once in ascending order:
that is the smaller number, 2345.

12
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:54.833
The same digits reversed are the larger,
5432. No second sort is needed.

13
00:00:55.633 --> 00:01:00.433
Now let's write it in Python: almost
the same code as with three digits.

14
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.800
The function is_special_4 takes x,
and returns 1 or 0.

15
00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:13.533
The format 04d writes x with four digits,
zeros on the left: 95 becomes 0095.

16
00:01:13.533 --> 00:01:17.533
sorted sorts the four characters
in ascending order, into a list.

17
00:01:19.967 --> 00:01:23.867
Joining the characters in ascending order
gives the smaller number.

18
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.033
The same characters reversed give
the larger, with no second sort.

19
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.300
Finally compare the difference with x,
and return 1 or 0.

20
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.467
Let's run the code on 3524,
beside the page box's column subtraction.

21
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:46.200
x = 3524: the digits in ascending order
are 2, 3, 4 and 5.

22
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.200
The smaller number is 2345.

23
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.200
And the larger is 5432.

24
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.600
5432 minus 2345 is 3087, not 3524:
the function returns 0.

25
00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.800
And what if we apply the same step again and again,
each time to the difference?

26
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.200
3524 gives 3087, 3087 gives 8352,
and 8352 gives 6174.

27
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:11.700
And there it stops: 7641 minus 1467 is
exactly 6174. On 6174 the function returns 1.

28
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:18.000
First trap: all digits equal, like
7777. The difference is 0, so 7777 is not special.

29
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.500
But 0 itself is special: 0000 minus 0000
is 0, the number itself. So says the page's table too.

30
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.800
Second trap: a short number. Without zeros
on the left, 95 gives 95 minus 59, that is 36.

31
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.600
Padded to four digits it is 0095:
9500 minus 0059, that is 9441.

32
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.000
The cost: always four digits,
so O(1) time and O(1) memory.

33
00:02:40.799 --> 00:02:45.999
Part B: run the function on 0 to 9999.
Which numbers are special, and why?

34
00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:50.599
Call the sorted digits,
from the largest a to the smallest d.

35
00:02:50.599 --> 00:02:55.399
The larger minus the smaller: the same digits,
once in one order and once reversed.

36
00:02:55.399 --> 00:02:59.999
This time the middle digits do not cancel,
but everything groups into two gaps.

37
00:02:59.999 --> 00:03:04.732
p is the gap between the largest and the smallest,
and q the gap between the two middle ones.

38
00:03:04.732 --> 00:03:08.766
The difference is 999p plus 90q:
it depends only on the pair p, q.

39
00:03:08.766 --> 00:03:14.266
b, c lie between a and d, so q ≤ p ≤ 9:
only 55 pairs, so only 55 differences.

40
00:03:15.066 --> 00:03:21.032
Each cell is one pair, holding the difference
it gives, 999p + 90q. p = 0 gives only 0.

41
00:03:21.032 --> 00:03:26.032
Check every cell: do the digits
of the difference give the same p and q again?

42
00:03:26.032 --> 00:03:30.632
53 cells fail: the digits
of their difference give another pair.

43
00:03:30.632 --> 00:03:34.666
Only one cell passes:
p = 6, q = 2, that is 6174.

44
00:03:34.666 --> 00:03:38.766
Together with 0, there are exactly two
special numbers: 0 and 6174.

45
00:03:39.566 --> 00:03:45.066
Why? Column subtraction gives the digits of the difference
straight from p and q, borrowing in the low columns.

46
00:03:45.066 --> 00:03:50.566
A special number equals its difference, so the difference's
digits have the same p and q: two conditions.

47
00:03:50.566 --> 00:03:56.066
When q = 0 the difference has two 9s, and then
it needs three. That fails: the column is out.

48
00:03:56.866 --> 00:04:01.832
When q ≥ 1, check which digit is the largest
and which the smallest: only seven cases.

49
00:04:01.832 --> 00:04:06.999
Six cases are ruled out. In the only one
left, the largest is 9 − q, the smallest q − 1.

50
00:04:06.999 --> 00:04:11.966
The second condition gives q = 2, so p = 6:
999 · 6 + 90 · 2 = 6174.

51
00:04:12.766 --> 00:04:15.999
And in code: no proof, just check everything.

52
00:04:18.199 --> 00:04:21.999
find_special_4 takes nothing,
and returns a list.

53
00:04:25.666 --> 00:04:30.166
It goes over x from 0 to 9999, and keeps
every x for which is_special_4 returns 1.

54
00:04:30.866 --> 00:04:35.466
x = 0: all digits are 0, and the difference is 0.
It is special, and goes into the list.

55
00:04:35.466 --> 00:04:39.566
From 1 to 6173 there is no special number,
so we jump ahead.

56
00:04:39.566 --> 00:04:44.232
x = 6174: 7641 minus 1467 is 6174.
Special, and it goes into the list.

57
00:04:44.232 --> 00:04:48.632
And from 6175 to 9999 again there is none:
the result is 0 and 6174.

58
00:04:49.432 --> 00:04:54.932
In the page's box you click a pair, and the arrow
leads to the pair of the next difference. Start from 9441.

59
00:04:54.932 --> 00:04:58.732
9441 gives the pair p = 8, q = 0,
that is 7992.

60
00:04:58.732 --> 00:05:01.866
7992 leads to 7173,

61
00:05:01.866 --> 00:05:04.999
7173 to 6354,

62
00:05:04.999 --> 00:05:08.132
6354 to 3087,

63
00:05:08.132 --> 00:05:11.266
and 3087 to 8352.

64
00:05:11.266 --> 00:05:16.232
8352 leads to 6174, and the pair (6, 2)
points to itself: the path stops.

65
00:05:16.832 --> 00:05:22.332
Over all 10,000 numbers: 9,990 reach
6174 within seven steps at most.

66
00:05:22.332 --> 00:05:26.332
And the ten numbers whose digits are all equal,
like 7777, fall to 0.

67
00:05:27.132 --> 00:05:32.632
The loop checks 10,000 numbers, each
in O(1). By the proof, checking 55 is enough.

68
00:05:33.432 --> 00:05:37.666
Part C, briefly: the same process,
but the number is written in base B.

69
00:05:38.366 --> 00:05:42.899
The same computation, with B instead of 10:
the difference again depends only on p and q.

70
00:05:42.899 --> 00:05:47.499
And its digits, when q ≥ 1, are p, q − 1,
B − q − 1 and B − p.

71
00:05:47.499 --> 00:05:52.999
The same two conditions. Only one split of
the digits gives a family: p = 3q and B = 5q.

72
00:05:53.799 --> 00:05:59.299
The answer: a fixed point exists when B is divisible
by 5; its digits: 3k, k − 1, 4k − 1, 2k.

73
00:05:59.299 --> 00:06:04.599
Base 5 gives 3032, base 15 gives 92B6,
and in base 10, with k = 2, it is 6174.

74
00:06:04.599 --> 00:06:10.099
And two exceptions: 0111 and 1001 in base 2,
and 3021 in base 4. No other base has one.

75
00:06:10.899 --> 00:06:16.366
But existence is not convergence. In the page's box:
base 15, and its fixed point 92B6.

76
00:06:16.366 --> 00:06:20.866
1234 in base 15 does not reach it: it enters
a cycle of four, 30DC to 9676.

77
00:06:20.866 --> 00:06:24.766
The same digits in base 10:
1234, with p = 3 and q = 1.

78
00:06:24.766 --> 00:06:28.732
Three steps, through 3087 and 8352,
and it stops at 6174.

79
00:06:29.532 --> 00:06:35.432
In short: the difference depends only on p and q,
and only the pair (6, 2) returns to itself: 6174.

80
00:06:35.432 --> 00:06:40.332
Try your own numbers and bases in the boxes
on the page of question 0013 on the site.
